---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

// These question bodies are hand-written HTML, so their hrefs need the site
// base prefixed the same way u() does it. Both fields carry links - applying it
// to only one of them left five links broken in the built site.
const base = (html) => html.replace(/href='\//g, `href='${u("/")}`);

const Q = [
  { n: 1, w: "answered", t: "Who were Ljubomir's parents? — ANSWERED",
    b: "This was the archive's central doubt: the join at generation three rested on a man with no document. The <a href='/marriage-1920'><strong>Senj marriage register of 8 December 1920</strong></a> names the groom's parents outright — <strong>Juraj Blažević and Tereza <em>r.</em> Žubrinić</strong> — and confirms Ljubomir's birth as 1 March 1892. It was attached in the family tree the whole time and absent from the GEDCOM export.",
    f: "Nothing further. <strong>What replaces it is question 1b.</strong>" },
  { n: "1b", w: "high", t: "Who was Juraj Blažević's father? — a house number, and a second witness",
    b: "Four more registers were read in September 2026 and the man is no longer a bare name.<br><br><strong>He has an occupation and a house.</strong> His son <strong>Marijan's baptism, 12 April 1895</strong>, enters him as <strong>“Jurko Blažević i Tereza rođ. Žubrinić, <em>selj.</em>”</strong> — peasant — living at <strong>Smokvica kbr. 114</strong>.<br><br><strong>That house is a zadruga, not a household — this archive's own correction.</strong> <a href='/people/toma-tomislav-blazevic'>Toma</a> and Terezija Pećanić are recorded at <strong>Smokvica 114</strong> in <strong>1882</strong> and <strong>1884</strong>, and Toma was born at <em>Br. 114</em> in 1838. But a control document — the 1890 baptism of Frane Blažević — puts <strong>Pavao Blažević, son of Luka</strong>, at Smokvica 114 as well. Luka and Toma's father Anton are both sons of <a href='/people/ilija-blazevic'>Ilija</a>, so Pavao and Toma are first cousins, and the number covers an <strong>extended-family compound</strong>. It places Juraj in Ilija's kin-group; it cannot name his father. <a href='/blazevic'>The zadruga →</a><br><br><strong>And a second register names the couple.</strong> <a href='/people/anka-blazevic'>Anka Blažević's</a> marriage to Pavao Kosina on <strong>20 November 1919</strong> gives her parents as <strong>Juraj Blažević i Tereza r. Žubrinić</strong> — independently of the 1920 entry for their son Ljubomir.",
    f: "<strong>And the godparents close the circle.</strong> The tree gives Juraj two younger siblings, <a href='/people/marija-blazevic-2'>Marija (b. 1880)</a> and <a href='/people/ivan-blazevic'>Ivan (b. 1882)</a> — children of Toma at house 114. The godparents at Marijan's 1895 baptism are entered as <strong>“Ive Blažević, <em>sin Tomin</em>”</strong> and <strong>“Marija Blažević, <em>ćer Tomina</em>”</strong>: the son and the daughter of a Toma, of the right names and the right ages, standing for the child of the man who lives in Toma's house. With the house number demoted to a kin-group, <strong>the godparents carry the weight</strong> — and they carry it well. Godparents are <em>chosen</em>, and a man's own brother and sister are the classic choice.<br><br>It is still not the sentence that says so. What would give it: <strong>Jurko's own baptism</strong> in the Sv. Jakov Krmpote register, or his <strong>marriage to Tereza Žubrinić</strong> before 1892. Two of his children, <strong>Marijan (1895)</strong> and <strong>Ruža (1897)</strong>, have no marriage recorded anywhere — and every marriage entry re-names the parents, so those are two more chances at the same answer." },
  { n: "1c", w: "answered", t: "Who was Milka's first husband? — ANSWERED",
    b: "<strong>Zvonimir M. Vukelić</strong>, <em>gostioničarski pomoćnik</em> — innkeeper's assistant — born 13 January 1883, married at Senj on <strong>4 November 1907</strong> (marriages p.294 no.30). He <strong>died in 1915</strong>, aged thirty-two, leaving Milka a widow at twenty-eight. Their children <strong>Miroslava “Mica” Vukelić (1907–1993)</strong> and <strong>Josip Vukelić (1913–2004)</strong> are <strong>Hedviga's half-sister and half-brother</strong> — and were in the tree all along. <a href='/milka'>Milka's life →</a>",
    f: "Nothing further on identity. <strong>What remains is 1d.</strong>" },
  { n: "1d", w: "medium", t: "How did Zvonimir Vukelić die in 1915? — and a correction",
    b: "This archive proposed the Austro-Hungarian casualty lists, reasoning that he was thirty-two in the second year of the war. <strong>That reasoning was probably wrong.</strong> The tree places his death <strong>at Senj</strong>, in his own town, and gives his occupation as <strong><em>gostioničar, bankar</em></strong> — innkeeper and <strong>banker</strong>, a considerable rise from the <em>gostioničarski pomoćnik</em> who married Milka in 1907. A man who dies at home, in a settled trade, is not a <em>Verlustliste</em> entry.<br><br>His baptism has since been read: born <strong>13 January 1883</strong> at Senj, baptised 25 February, son of <strong>Petar <em>pl.</em> Vukelić</strong> — <em>plemeniti</em>, <strong>noble</strong> — and Lojza. Milka married into a titled family and was widowed out of it at twenty-eight.",
    f: "<strong>And a third occupation, from a third document.</strong> On 16 August 1914 he stands witness at the wedding of Milka's sister Franjka, entered as <strong>“Zvonko M. Vukelić, <em>pivar</em>”</strong> — brewer. The trajectory now reads <em>gostioničarski pomoćnik</em> (1907) → <em>pivar</em> (1914) → <em>gostioničar, bankar</em> (at death). It is also the first document found that places him inside this family other than his own marriage entry.<br><br>The <strong>Senj death register for 1915</strong>, and nothing else. The casualty lists are behind a subscription for uncommon surnames and are, on this evidence, the wrong book." },
  { n: "1e", w: "medium", t: "Which came first — the wedding or the child?",
    b: "Milka and Zvonimir married on 4 November 1907 <strong>by episcopal dispensation, with the banns read once on 3 November</strong> rather than three times. Their daughter Miroslava was born in <strong>1907</strong>. A dispensation, a one-day wedding and a child in the same year make a familiar shape — but the register does not date the birth, and this archive does not claim to know.",
    f: "<strong>Miroslava Vukelić's baptismal entry, Senj 1907</strong>, which gives the day." },
  { n: 2, w: "critical", t: "How did Oto Blažević die?",
    b: "Two accounts in the same note, from two named relatives: <strong>a snakebite</strong>, and <strong>killed in the war in Slavonia with no known grave</strong>. A photograph of <a href='/grave'>the family grave</a> now corroborates the second witness in its checkable parts — Oto's name is on the stone, and Leo and Katica were added later exactly as she described. It still does not establish the <em>cause</em>. <a href='/oto'>The full page →</a>",
    f: "A search of the <strong>Yugoslav war victims register</strong> (<em>Popis žrtava rata</em>) and Croatian State Archives military death records for <strong>Oto Blažević, b. 1923, Senj</strong>. An entry — or a confirmed absence — settles it." },
  { n: 3, w: "high", t: "When did Santou Codron leave Rhodes?",
    b: "Born on Rhodes in <strong>1913</strong>; alive in South Africa to marry Hedviga about <strong>1973</strong>. The island's Jewish community was deported on 23 July 1944 and roughly 150 of 1,615 survived. Whether he left before the war — as ~2,000 Rhodes Jews did, for the Congo and Rhodesia — or survived the deportation is <strong>not known here</strong>, and the difference is enormous. <a href='/rhodes'>The full page →</a>",
    f: "South African <strong>immigration and naturalisation papers</strong> giving arrival year and last residence; the <strong>Rhodes Jewish Museum</strong> community registers and <strong>Yad Vashem</strong> deportation lists, searched for Codron." },
  { n: 4, w: "critical", t: "Is the Port Pirie branch the same Žubrinić family?",
    b: "Now traceable three generations back from the emigrant: <a href='/people/joannes-juan-ivan-ive-john-zubrinic'>Ivan Žubrinić</a> (1860–1930) → his father <strong>Joannes, b. 19 June 1837, Forest Ranger</strong> → <strong>Elias “Ilija” (1806–1845)</strong> → <strong>Casparus (1767–1829)</strong>. And there it stops: in the tree, Casparus's parent is a node literally named <em class='mono'>“Brothers of Casparus (It Seems) Žubrinić”</em> — a researcher's guess written into the structure. <strong>Nothing joins the two lines.</strong> Casparus (b. 14 Jan 1767) and our <a href='/people/toma-zubrinic'>Toma</a> (b. 1768) are born a year apart in the same Frontier district, in a surname held by ~312 people on earth. <a href='/frontier'>The Frontier →</a>",
    f: "The <strong>Otočac parish registers for the 1760s</strong> — the baptisms of Casparus (14 January 1767) and Toma (1768), two entries a few pages apart, which would name one father or two." },
  { n: "4b", w: "medium", t: "What killed Elias Žubrinić and Ana Untersinger in 1845?",
    b: "Both are given <strong>1845</strong>: he was thirty-eight, she thirty-seven, and they left seven children between about one and fifteen — including the eight-year-old who became the Otočac forest ranger and fathered the Port Pirie emigrant. Two adults dying in one year suggests an epidemic or a famine in 1840s Lika rather than coincidence. The tree gives no day, no cause and no source.",
    f: "The <strong>Otočac death register for 1845</strong>. Also worth knowing: <strong>Untersinger</strong> is a German surname in Lika, which the Frontier's officer and administrative class would explain — and which nothing here has traced." },
  { n: "4d", w: "critical", t: "Which Maria Orešković, and was there a second wife?",
    b: "<strong>That Maria Catharina is the wrong woman was already established</strong> — her own 1835 baptism names her parents as the tree does and places her at <em>Dubrava N° 29</em>, so a real person has simply been married to the wrong man. <a href='/frontier'>That is set out on the Frontier page.</a> This question is what reading a <em>second</em> register did to it.<br><br><strong>1834.</strong> Nicolaus is baptised 24 January, <em>filius legitimus</em>, <em>Confiniarius ex Pago Shumechicza N. 24</em>, to <strong>Michaël Xubrinich et Maria uxor ejus <em>nata Oreskovich</em></strong>. A married woman bearing a legitimate child — <strong>nineteen months before Maria Catharina was born</strong>. She is a different woman, born around 1810–15, and this archive knows nothing else about her.<br><br><strong>1845.</strong> Josephus is baptised 16 March to <strong>Michaël et Maria Žubrinić <em>nata Marković</em></strong>. Either the first Maria died and he married a second Maria, or there are <strong>two men of this name</strong> — and with 68 Žubrinić records at Otočac that is not far-fetched.<br><br>The consequence is larger than one person: <strong>ahnentafel 21's entire ancestry is attached to the wrong woman</strong> — Marcus Orešković, Lucia Sekula, and everything above them.",
    f: "Two books, both at Otočac. The <strong>marriage register</strong>, for a Žubrinić–Orešković marriage before 1834 and a Žubrinić–Marković marriage between 1834 and 1845 — one entry or two answers the whole question. And the <strong>death register</strong>, for a Maria Žubrinić <em>née Orešković</em> in that eleven-year window. <a href='/changed'>Why this was reclassified →</a>" },
  { n: "4c", w: "medium", t: "Can 232 Žubrinići be joined into one family?",
    b: "The tree holds <strong>232</strong> people of the name and they resolve into <strong>94 separate root ancestors</strong> — 94 people with no recorded Žubrinić parent. Given a surname with about 312 living bearers worldwide and a single district of origin, <strong>most of these fragments are almost certainly one family</strong>. Joining them is the largest single piece of work outstanding in this archive.",
    f: "A systematic read of the <strong>Otočac registers</strong> (and Prozor, Šumećica, Kompolje), rather than record-by-record hint-following. This is a job for someone who can work in Latin parish hands and knows that <strong>X spells Ž</strong> before about 1850." },
  { n: 5, w: "high", t: "Are Antić and Papić one family or two?",
    b: "The Selce ancestor is entered as <a href='/people/georgius-juraj-joannis-papa-antic-papic'>“Georgius Juraj (Joannis) <em>Papa</em> ‘Jure’ <strong>Antić Papić</strong>”</a>, carrying two surnames at once; his father and grandfather are plain <strong>Antić</strong>. Seventy-nine people in the tree are written <em>Papić (Papa)</em>, and one record hedges as <em>“Papić or Perpic”</em>. Either a family changed its name, or two families have been merged." ,
    f: "The <strong>Selce parish registers</strong> across 1765–1838, reading the surname as the priest wrote it in each generation.<br><br><strong>September 2026 — the register supplies the mechanism.</strong> The Vrbnik baptism of <a href='/people/josephum-antic'>Josephum Antić</a>, 14 March 1806, enters his mother as <strong>“Matre Marthæ Antich <u>Papa</u>”</strong> — and the scribe <em>underlines</em> the second word. He does it throughout the page: <em>Loncsarich <u>Franich</u></em>, <em>Antich <u>Dudin</u></em>, <em>Pobor <u>Rataj</u></em>. These are <strong>nadimci</strong>, house by-names that tell one household from another where a village shares a surname — the same job the <a href='/gazetteer'>house numbers</a> do at Smokvica.<br><br>So <em>Papa</em> is documented as <strong>the by-name of an Antić household</strong>, not as a surname. That does not yet prove the 79 people written <em>Papić (Papa)</em> are Antići — but it names the mechanism by which a by-name becomes a surname, and it is the first evidence in the question that is not a guess." },
  { n: "5b", w: "high", t: "Two sisters born eighteen days apart",
    b: "<a href='/people/franka-papic'>Franjka Papić</a> and <a href='/people/sulka-papic'>Šulka Papić</a> are both daughters of <strong>Josip Papić and Antonija <em>r.</em> Prpić</strong> — Milka's parents, so both are <a href='/hedviga'>Hedviga's</a> aunts. Franjka's marriage register (Senj, 16 August 1914) gives her birth as <strong>20 January 1895</strong>. Šulka's (Senj, 21 December 1919) gives hers as <strong>7 February 1895</strong>. That is <strong>eighteen days</strong>, and they cannot both be right for full sisters.<br><br>Neither date is this archive's inference: each is written in that woman's own entry. A marriage register records <em>what the bride said</em>, not what the baptism book says, so either could be the loose one. They might be twins with garbled dates; one might belong to a different Papić couple, and there were at least three having children in Senj in these years.",
    f: "<strong>It is not a tree error.</strong> All four Papić sisters' marriage registers have now been read, and the tree reproduces every one of them exactly: <a href='/people/ana-papic'>Ana</a> 25 May 1888, <a href='/people/bozica-tonka-papic'>Božica</a> 9 December 1891, Franjka 20 January 1895, Šulka 7 February 1895. So the <em>registers</em> disagree with each other and the tree faithfully carries both — which moves the question off the transcription and onto the books.<br><br>The <strong>Senj baptism register for January–February 1895</strong> settles it. Both entries would fall within three weeks of each other in the same book, and it would take one sitting." },
  { n: 6, w: "medium", t: "Why is Antonija Prpić buried at Mirogoj?",
    b: "She was born at <strong>Krivi Put 136</strong> in 1859 and died at <strong>Senj</strong> in 1935 — but is buried at <strong>Mirogoj in Zagreb</strong>, 200 km away. Nothing in the record explains it. A child living in Zagreb is the obvious guess and it is only a guess.",
    f: "The <strong>Mirogoj burial register</strong>, which gives the grave, the date and who paid for it." },
  { n: 7, w: "medium", t: "Two deaths on 30 October, six years apart",
    b: "Ivan Anton Defranceski died at Senj on <strong>30 October 1995</strong>; Hedviga died at Johannesburg on <strong>30 October 2001</strong>. Both dates come from the tree, not from certificates seen here. A shared date is exactly what memory tidies into place.",
    f: "Both <strong>death certificates</strong> — Croatian civil registration for Senj 1995, South African for Johannesburg 2001." },
  { n: 8, w: "medium", t: "What was the Senj tobacco factory?",
    b: "Two women of this family worked there — <a href='/people/milka-lucia-papic'>Milka</a> as a supervisor, <strong>Anka Blažević</strong> (1893–1953) on the floor. The tree gives the building's address and says it is now a textile factory. Its founding date, size and closure are unconfirmed here; the machinery is said to have gone to <strong>Rovinj</strong> after the Second World War.",
    f: "The <strong>Senj town museum</strong> and Croatian industrial-history literature on the state tobacco monopoly after the patent of 20 November 1850.<br><br><strong>Its scale can now be read off one page.</strong> Senj marriages p.359 covers 20 November – 22 December 1919, entries 44 to 51. <strong>Six of the eight brides</strong> — Anka Blažević, Marija Ilić, Ivka Babić, Marija Babić, Božica Zrinski and <a href='/people/sulka-papic'>Šulka Papić</a> — are entered <em>radnica u tvornici duhana</em>, and entry 45's witness Ivan Antić is a <em>mašinista</em> in it.<br><br>Three pages across three registers, counted the same way:<br><br><span class='mono'>p.303 · 1909 · 3 of 5 brides<br>p.350 · 1918 · 3 of 6 brides<br>p.359 · 1919 · 6 of 8 brides</span><br><br>This is not one unusual page. Across at least 1909–1919 the factory is the standing occupation of the young women marrying in Senj, and men appear in it too — <strong>Mate Tadić</strong>, <em>radnik u tvornici duhana</em>, and <strong>Ivan Antić</strong>, <em>mašinista</em>." },
  { n: 9, w: "data", t: "Three place errors that will mislead someone",
    b: "<strong>(a)</strong> Juraj Blažević's birthplace is geocoded to <em>Smokvica, Općina Smokvica, Dubrovačko-neretvanska županija</em> — that is <strong>Smokvica on Korčula</strong>, 400 km south. The village meant is <strong>Smokvica Krmpotska</strong> near Novi Vinodolski. <strong>(b)</strong> Ivan Žubrinić of Port Pirie is given <em>“Senj, Split-Dalmatia”</em>; Senj is in <strong>Lika-Senj</strong>. <strong>(c)</strong> A FamilySearch citation for Nicolaus Žubrinić's Otočac christening is catalogued under <em>“Osekovo, Belovár-Körös Megye, Hungary”</em>.",
    f: "Correcting (a) and (b) in the MyHeritage tree at source. (c) is FamilySearch's error, not the tree's — but it should be noted wherever the citation is reused." },
  { n: 10, w: "answered", t: "The photographs are not in the export — ANSWERED",
    b: "The GEDCOM contains no media records at all. <strong>Seventy items</strong> have now been swept out of the MyHeritage media library across the whole line — 34 of them register scans, certificates and gravestones, many at 4,500 px. Three are read out in full: <a href='/marriage-1920'>the 1920 marriage register</a>, <a href='/certificate'>the 1951 birth extract</a>, <a href='/grave'>the family grave</a>. <a href='/pictures'>All of them →</a>",
    f: "Nothing further for the direct line. The same sweep has <strong>not</strong> been run on the wider register of 1,035 people, and the site library holds about <strong>4,500 items</strong> in total." },
  { n: 11, w: "high", t: "Who is in the photographs?",
    b: "Most of the recovered photographs carry <strong>no caption but a camera filename</strong> — <em>1595624858.9990501</em>, <em>1134835622</em>, <em>IMG_5752</em>. Faces this archive can name are guesses from resemblance and from the ages of children in shot. <a href='/pictures'>The pictures →</a>",
    f: "Somebody in the family looking at them. This is the one open question here that <strong>no archive can close</strong> and a single phone call could — so the pictures have been gathered onto <a href='/faces'>a single contact sheet</a> meant to be put in front of a relative, with what is being asked of each one spelled out.<br><br><strong>And five of them were not photographs.</strong> Five items were being published as portraits of ancestors born between 1806 and 1838; they are stock question-mark placeholder graphics, one with a visible Adobe Stock watermark. They are now excluded from the build. <a href='/faces'>The five →</a>" },
  { n: 12, w: "answered", t: "Why does Ivan Defranceski have Arolsen card files? — ANSWERED",
    b: "They are his, beyond reasonable doubt: all three give the birth as <strong>25 June 1925 at Crikvenica</strong>, matching the <a href='/certificate'>1951 civil birth extract</a> exactly. They place him at <strong>Licht und Kraft, Reutlingen</strong>, as an <em>Hilfsarbeiter</em> — unskilled labourer — from <strong>21 October 1941, aged sixteen</strong>, lodging at Brunnenstraße 7, Pfullingen, with a second Reutlingen employer from January 1942. <a href='/arolsen'>The full reading →</a>",
    f: "Nothing further on identity. <strong>What replaces it is 12b and 12c.</strong>" },
  { n: "12b", w: "critical", t: "Was he a forced labourer or a free one?",
    b: "The 1950 municipal questionnaires carry three printed lines designed to answer exactly this — <em>Wurde der Arbeiter restlos entlohnt?</em> (was he paid in full), <em>Dienstverpflichtet</em> (compulsorily assigned), <em>Freier Arbeiter … seit</em> (free worker, since). <strong>All three are blank on both cards.</strong> The collection is titled a card file of <em>persecutees</em>, but that is the name of a zone-wide registration file, not a finding about anyone in it. <strong>This archive claims neither.</strong>",
    f: "A direct enquiry to the <strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> for the full file on Defranceski, b. 25.6.1925 — they answer families without charge, and the online collections may hold employment or transport records these three cards were extracted from." },
  { n: "12c", w: "high", t: "How long was he in Germany?",
    b: "The second employment runs from <strong>26 January 1942</strong> to <strong>28 November 194?</strong> — the last digit of the year runs into the column rule and cannot be read. <strong>1942 gives ten months in Germany; 1944 gives just over three years.</strong> His daughter is born at Crikvenica in 1948, so he was home by then, and nothing at all is documented between the card and that birth.",
    f: "A higher-resolution scan of the insurance card, or the Arolsen file itself. The second employer's name is also unread — an old German hand, possibly beginning <em>Otto</em>." },
  { n: 13, w: "data", t: "The gravestone contradicts the register",
    b: "The <a href='/grave'>Senj stone</a> gives Ljubomir's birth as <strong>1891</strong>. The <a href='/marriage-1920'>1920 marriage register</a>, written in his lifetime from his baptismal certificate, gives <strong>1 March 1892</strong>. This archive follows the register.",
    f: "The <strong>Rijeka baptismal entry of 1892</strong> would settle it beyond argument — and would also independently name his parents a second time." },
];
const cls = { critical: "warn", high: "", medium: "", data: "dim" };
---
<Base title="Open Questions"
  eyebrow={`${Q.filter((q) => q.w !== "answered").length} open · ${Q.filter((q) => q.w === "answered").length} answered`}
  dek="Written down so they can be worked on, and so no reader mistakes a gap for a fact. Each one says what would actually close it."
>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The most valuable thing anyone can send this archive is a
    <strong>correction</strong>. The second most valuable is a document that closes one of
    these.</p>
  </section>

  {Q.map((q) => (
    <section class="blk qa">
      <div class="lab">{q.w === "answered" ? "Answered — kept so the change is visible"
        : q.w === "critical" ? "Critical — the spine depends on it"
        : q.w === "data" ? "Data quality" : q.w === "high" ? "Substantive" : "Worth doing"}</div>
      <h2><span class="mono dim">{q.n}.</span> {q.t}</h2>
      <p set:html={base(q.b)} />
      <div class="note"><strong>What would settle it: </strong>
        <span set:html={base(q.f)} /></div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Drafted, not sent</div>
    <h2>Two letters</h2>
    <p class="lede">Two of these questions can only be answered by a book that is not online. The
    enquiries are written and sit in <code class="mono">requests/</code>, each naming exactly what is
    wanted and what is already known, so the search can be narrow.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Državni arhiv<br />u Gospiću</th><td>The registers of <strong>Sv. Jakov,
        Krmpote</strong> — the parish named by Toma's 1907 death entry, and therefore the book
        holding every Blažević above him. Wanted: <strong>Jurko's baptism</strong> (likely
        1855–1870), his <strong>marriage to Tereza Žubrinić</strong> (likely 1885–92), or his
        <strong>death</strong> before December 1920. Any one of the three names his father and ends
        question 1b.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Senj, 1915</th><td>The <strong>death entry of Zvonimir Vukelić</strong> — how Milka
        came to be widowed at twenty-eight, about which this archive knows nothing at all.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem">Both drafts say plainly that <strong>a nil return is
    worth having</strong> and will be recorded as one. An archive that holds nothing is a finding;
    an archive never asked is not.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the standing one</h2>
    <p class="note">Everything above generation eight is <strong>silence</strong>, not mystery. The
    Croatian parish registers for this coast begin in the sixteenth century and many survive; the
    Blažević line simply has not been followed into them below <strong>Mateša Blažević, born about
    1750</strong>. That is a research gap, and it is closable by someone with the patience to read
    Latin parish hands. <a href={u("/how-far-back")}>How far back →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
